#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088550} :activity/id #uuid "786899da-3901-5475-83a3-ec44a5b8e0bf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048432 :topic/id #uuid "9ed8efeb-9446-5c06-a1db-8b5307aeb373" :topic/math-academy-id 853} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088901 :tutorial/content "An **ellipse** is a circle that has been stretched. For example, let's look at the ellipse given below:\n\n![](images/ce/ce2c4115b8df804bacf39067bb8cf29f773226291d9e7d7bd06b68b2d689fbb2.png)\n\nThe longest diameter of an ellipse is called the **major axis**.\n\nSo, for the ellipse above, the major axis is the line segment bounded by the points $(1,-2)$ and $(1,4)$, and its length is $4-(-2)=6$.\n\nHalf of the major axis is called a **semi-major axis**. For our ellipse above, the length of the semi-major axis is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{2} = 3\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the **minor axis** is the shortest diameter of an ellipse.\nLet's have another look at the same ellipse:\n\n![](images/94/945d17d751336dad4d5b4b30fa8cbffb51c0c8f96c7b919e7cf3d3674162f209.png)\n\nHere, we see the minor axis of the ellipse. It is the line segment bounded by the points $(-1,1)$ and $(3,1)$, and its length is $3-(-1)=4$.\n\nHalf of the minor axis is called a **semi-minor axis**. For our ellipse, the semi-minor axis has a length of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{2} = 2\n$$\n\nFor this particular ellipse:\n\n- Since the major axis is parallel to the $y$-axis, the major axis is said to be \"along the $y$-direction\".\n- Since the minor axis is parallel to the $x$-axis, the minor axis is said to be \"along the $x$-direction\".\n\nNote that for other ellipses, it may be opposite: the major axis may be along the $x$-direction, and the minor axis may be along the $y$-direction." :tutorial/id #uuid "75014092-bc8b-5d47-8524-85ded52c6a1f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3346 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "82a2e40f-2e37-590f-b29f-5fbd95129189" :step/math-academy-id 18537 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088551}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088551 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087512 :question/id #uuid "7b791a72-d6b9-5fab-ab7e-d048452e552c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5068"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed1761-ceeb-5033-afa2-cb735e6bb96c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049868}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233216}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes of an Ellipse"} :step/id #uuid "8f0a6df7-c5db-553a-b243-d6e5bcc7a010" :step/math-academy-id 18538 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088552}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087513 :question/id #uuid "6d0850ce-8494-5805-9676-3a3c1675783e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5069"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a9920da-57b0-566d-8ce5-5e94d0de2789" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088453}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Semi-Major Axis of an Ellipse"} :step/id #uuid "9df4a6b4-20a2-506e-b699-ab3af47805bb" :step/math-academy-id 18539 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088553}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087716 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087514 :question/id #uuid "91e00152-6173-5382-b28d-416a40625254" :question/math-academy-id "e-5070"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0220e207-6d31-5019-a830-111addfe4741" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088454}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Semi-Minor Axis of an Ellipse"} :step/id #uuid "5d16b832-01d0-5168-848f-6f59965f5b13" :step/math-academy-id 18541}] :